WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.920
Jennette McCurdy was a popular face on Nickelodeon&nbsp;
in the 2000s, but for a number of reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.440
she's stepped back from the spotlight&nbsp;
in recent years. Here's why she's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.640
getting cast much anymore and what&nbsp;
the future has in store for her.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.520
In March 2014, three racy photos&nbsp;
of McCurdy were leaked online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.640
which put the future of her Nickelodeon show Sam&nbsp;
&amp; Cat in jeopardy. McCurdy seemed to imply that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:29.120
the culprit behind the leak was NBA player Andre&nbsp;
Drummond, whom she dated in 2013, as she tweeted,

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.320
"To anyone disappointed: I&nbsp;
sent those pics to 1 person.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:37.520
You can connect the dots. Shocked someone would&nbsp;
stoop so low. I just speak [with] candor."

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.960
When people started pointing fingers&nbsp;
at Drummond, he tweeted in response,

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.280
"For all the speculations about leaking anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.440
I have nothing to do with it. I'm&nbsp;
focused on basketball. Thank you."

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.240
Ultimately, the leaker's identity was never&nbsp;
definitively revealed. McCurdy later took to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.880
Reddit to address the leaked photos as well as&nbsp;
some recent Instagram selfies, as she wrote,

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.440
"I post to social media when I want to and&nbsp;
how I want to and I would whether I was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.640
the entertainment industry or not. I&nbsp;
don't believe people should attack me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.640
because their image of me is destroyed&nbsp;
after I post a harmless, sultry photo."

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:09.440
She added,

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:13.440
"I don't believe an actor/actress should lose&nbsp;
the rights to be themselves in an attempt to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.800
cater to the image that was created by&nbsp;
a character they played. that's all."

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.040
Ultimately, Sam &amp; Cat was canceled&nbsp;
after just one season not because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.120
McCurdy's leaked photos but because of her&nbsp;
rocky relationship with Nickelodeon. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.280
rumors circulated that her co-star Ariana&nbsp;
Grande was being paid more than McCurdy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.080
Grande cleared the air on TwitLonger by saying,

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.320
"Jennette and I agreed upfront that we would&nbsp;
be treated equally on this show in all regards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.240
(as we should be, considering we each work&nbsp;
just as hard as the other on this show)."

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.040
Fans then speculated that McCurdy&nbsp;
and Grande didn't get along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.360
but as McCurdy explained to E! News,

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.760
"I just feel that Ariana and I were&nbsp;
and are extremely close and extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.360
like-minded in a lot of different ways,&nbsp;
and then, sorta as the show dissolved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.880
everybody wanted to find some sort of&nbsp;
hidden meaning in our relationship."

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:57.680
She added,

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.240
"I think we butted heads at times but in a&nbsp;
very sisterly way. Like she knows me so well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.400
and I know her so well that I think it was&nbsp;
unfortunate that things got misconstrued."

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:12.160
After Sam &amp; Cat, McCurdy moved on to a four-part&nbsp;
web series called What's Next for Sarah?, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:17.600
she wrote, co-edited, executive produced, and&nbsp;
starred in. It premiered on Vimeo on August 13th,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.240
2014. Taking inspiration from McCurdy's&nbsp;
own life, she played Sarah, an actress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:26.720
whose world begins to fall apart after a hit&nbsp;
show she stars on gets canceled. Many viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:31.600
speculated that a self-absorbed character&nbsp;
named Gloriana was based on Ariana Grande,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.240
partly because of a physical resemblance. McCurdy&nbsp;
never admitted that the character was a direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.880
reference to her old co-star, but she did address&nbsp;
the subject during an interview with ABC News.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.800
"So I'll ask about Gloriana then."

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:47.840
"Oh, yeah. That was definitely a character&nbsp;
that was heightened for comedic purposes."

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.960
McCurdy further explained that there&nbsp;
were blogs that blew the whole thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.920
out of proportion and that she was&nbsp;
just trying to make a joke out of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.920
own experience. She also talked about her&nbsp;
relationship with Grande, as she revealed,

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.520
"We've had coffee since this web series and&nbsp;
everything, we've talked. She's awesome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.640
and I wish her all the best, and I&nbsp;
know that she does the same for me."

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.240
Another major factor in McCurdy's&nbsp;
break from acting was the loss of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.720
her mother Debbie in 2013. In&nbsp;
1995, Debbie was diagnosed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.560
breast cancer and initially beat it back&nbsp;
with treatment. But fifteen years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.440
the cancer had returned and spread. As McCurdy&nbsp;
wrote in The Wall Street Journal in 2011,

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.160
"I can't imagine how difficult it must be for my&nbsp;
mother to tolerate all she has gone through and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.880
continues to go through — the pain, the worry&nbsp;
and the battle. She wakes up every day hurting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:35.280
and goes to sleep hurting even more. My&nbsp;
mother, the constant optimist and effervescent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.480
sprightly woman I know her to be, is caged&nbsp;
along with this hideous beast they call cancer."

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:45.520
In 2015, McCurdy told Vulture that instead&nbsp;
of mourning, she worked. As she put it,

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.920
"Six days after my mom died I went back to&nbsp;
work. I thought, 'Oh if I do this Vine video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.080
or if I do this episode, if I hit every mark, then&nbsp;
I'll be fine because I'll have something else to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.720
focus on.' But really I was just putting this huge&nbsp;
life-changing event on the back burner and it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.360
slowly causing me to unravel to the point where&nbsp;
I would say I got to my lowest emotional point."

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.960
Throughout her career, McCurdy has&nbsp;
suffered from eating disorders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.920
which she opened up about in a 2019 essay&nbsp;
in The Huffington Post. As she explained,

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.000
"My disordered eating started when I was&nbsp;
11. As a child actress working in Hollywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.160
I quickly learned that remaining physically small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:22.240
for my age meant I had a better chance&nbsp;
of booking more roles. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:27.920
I had a trusty and dedicated companion ready to&nbsp;
help me with my burgeoning anorexia: my mom!"

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:28.800
She added,

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.760
"I don't hold this against my mom at&nbsp;
all. I don't think she could help it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:36.080
Mom had been hospitalized for anorexia on&nbsp;
several occasions when she was a teenager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:39.280
and I'm not convinced she ever&nbsp;
overcame her disordered eating."

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:44.480
McCurdy's anorexia would turn into binge eating&nbsp;
and eventually bulimia. After experiencing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:49.040
several come-to-Jesus moments such as losing&nbsp;
a tooth and popping blood vessels in her eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:53.520
she finally decided that she needed help and&nbsp;
met an eating disorder specialist. She'd already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.480
worked with a therapist a year and a half earlier,&nbsp;
but this time, she was committed. As she wrote,

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:02.800
"The important thing was getting back on the&nbsp;
recovery program anytime I had a slip so that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:06.000
as they say in recovery, 'the&nbsp;
slip doesn't become a slide.'"

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:11.680
"I suggest finding something, whatever your&nbsp;
passion is, whatever your hobby is, whatever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.400
just finding something that will&nbsp;
satisfy you and fulfill you."

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.200
After the death of her mother,&nbsp;
McCurdy felt lost and turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:21.360
to alcohol. As she explained on&nbsp;
her podcast Empty Inside in 2020,

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.320
"I'd be drunk in the middle of the&nbsp;
street in France. I was supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:27.840
to do a press junket the next&nbsp;
day. I got completely wasted."

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:32.320
In another episode of Empty Inside, McCurdy&nbsp;
discussed her struggle with obsessive-compulsive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:36.320
disorder and how it was related to her&nbsp;
Mormon upbringing. As she explained,

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:39.760
"In the Mormon church, they teach you&nbsp;
about this thing called the 'still small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:43.680
voice' [...] you're supposed to listen for it&nbsp;
and it kind of guides you to do what's right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:47.200
For me, I confused my anxiety&nbsp;
with the still small voice."

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:48.000
She added,

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.160
"I had OCD as a kid and I'd be like 'Okay,&nbsp;
so my still small voice is telling me that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:55.600
I need to touch this thing ten times&nbsp;
before I touch this other thing ten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:59.440
times so that I can then go to the door and&nbsp;
open this five times because the still small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:03.280
voice is telling that I need to do these&nbsp;
things or else my mom's going to die.'"

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:07.680
In her Huffington Post essay about her&nbsp;
eating disorders, McCurdy explained that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:12.160
she didn't want to be a role model during her&nbsp;
Nickelodeon days on iCarly. As she put it,

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:15.360
"I had [...] become acutely aware&nbsp;
that I was a role model for kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:18.880
which I felt like I was supposed to&nbsp;
find cool but actually found upsetting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:23.120
My great 'contribution' to society was&nbsp;
walking onto an overlit Nickelodeon set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:26.480
shouting lines about fried chicken [...]&nbsp;
and that's what kids were looking up to?"

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:28.560
"Can I have a chicken puck?"

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:31.400
"You want one of my chicken pucks?"

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:32.400
"Uh-huh."

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:33.360
"Just one puck?"

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:33.860
"Yeah."

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:35.220
"No."

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:37.840
McCurdy further explained,

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:41.600
"There was something about the shallowness&nbsp;
of my success that made me resent it. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.320
resentment festered, providing even more fuel for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:49.120
my disordered eating. I actively began&nbsp;
to engage in anorexic behavior again."

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:53.840
Obviously, not everyone has to be perfect, but&nbsp;
McCurdy found it especially absurd to be seen as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:58.000
a role model for playing an obnoxious character&nbsp;
on a children's show. Not to mention that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.160
was all going on while her mother was dying&nbsp;
and she was suffering from disordered eating.

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:07.840
In December 2020, it was reported that an iCarly&nbsp;
revival with most of the original cast was set to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:12.640
stream on Paramount+. But one cast member not&nbsp;
returning was Jennette McCurdy. Eight months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:17.520
earlier, McCurdy told Elite Daily that she doesn't&nbsp;
look back fondly on the show. As she put it,

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:21.680
"I still have complicated feelings toward my&nbsp;
past from my experience with Nickelodeon. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:26.400
something that I work on. I'd like to get to a&nbsp;
place of peace with my past. That would be great."

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:29.440
On the bright side, though, McCurdy&nbsp;
was at least happy for the time she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:32.240
spent with her iCarly co-workers, as she noted,

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:35.360
"I would not have such an enjoyable&nbsp;
life if these people weren't in it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:38.720
The human connections you have with&nbsp;
people is always what determines whether,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:41.600
ultimately, something is&nbsp;
a good or bad experience."

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:45.280
While McCurdy has mixed feelings about&nbsp;
her iCarly days, it's at least heartening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:48.240
to hear about the special connections&nbsp;
she made during her time on the show.

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:53.360
Beginning in 2015, McCurdy starred on&nbsp;
the Canadian sci-fi series Between.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.280
It ran for two seasons, and after it wasn't&nbsp;
renewed for a third, she decided to take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:01.600
break from acting and focus on other projects.&nbsp;
This was ultimately part of an effort to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:06.480
on something she could truly be happy about.&nbsp;
As she told The Hollywood Reporter in 2018,

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:10.560
"I never got the chance to be cast in a&nbsp;
project I was proud to be part of. Now I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:14.800
have a better chance of making things I'm proud&nbsp;
of than getting cast in things I'm proud of."

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:19.040
McCurdy assembled an all-female crew as she&nbsp;
wrote and directed the short film Kenny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:23.680
which was inspired by her mother's death.&nbsp;
It premiered in 2018 on Short of the Week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:27.840
a YouTube channel that showcases short films&nbsp;
from around the world. McCurdy has also written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:31.600
and directed other short films, like The&nbsp;
McCurdys, which is based on her childhood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:34.880
and Strong Independent Women,&nbsp;
which covers eating disorders.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:40.080
In 2020, McCurdy started hosting the podcast&nbsp;
Empty Inside, which covers many personal topics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:44.880
such as stage parents, rejection, and dating. Her&nbsp;
guests have included the likes of former American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:49.840
Idol contestant David Archuleta, filmmaker&nbsp;
Zane Rubin, and comedian Taylor Tomlinson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:53.840
McCurdy may not be acting much these days, but&nbsp;
she appears to be doing what makes her happy.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.440
In a February 2021 episode of Empty Inside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:02.320
McCurdy officially announced that she wouldn't&nbsp;
return to acting anytime soon. As she put it,

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:06.320
"I quit a few years ago because I initially&nbsp;
didn't want to do it. My mom put me in it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:10.800
when I was 6 and by [...] age [...] 10 or 11,&nbsp;
I was the main financial support for my family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:15.040
and it was very much just like the pressure of my&nbsp;
family didn't have a lot of money, and this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:19.520
kind of the way out, which I actually think was&nbsp;
helpful in driving me to some degree of success."

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:22.640
McCurdy also noted that acting was&nbsp;
difficult because of her nerves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:26.560
during auditions and that she lost much of&nbsp;
her interest in acting after her mom died.

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:31.440
"With her death kind of died a lot&nbsp;
of her ideas for my life and that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.840
its own journey and a difficult one, for sure."

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:40.640
She also reiterated that she feels ashamed of&nbsp;
many roles that she played. As she explained,

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:44.640
"I resent my career in a lot of ways. I&nbsp;
feel so unfulfilled by the roles that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:48.400
played and felt like it was the most&nbsp;
cheesy, embarrassing. I did the shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:53.920
that I was on from like 13 to 21, and by 15,&nbsp;
I was already embarrassed. My friends at 15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:57.600
they're not like, 'Oh, cool, you're on this&nbsp;
Nickelodeon show.' It was embarrassing."

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:00.960
We're sad to hear that McCurdy has no&nbsp;
intentions of acting again anytime soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:03.840
but we're glad that she's now&nbsp;
choosing to do what makes her happy.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:08.160
If you or someone you know is struggling with&nbsp;
addiction, please call the Substance Abuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:17.840
and Mental Health Administration's 24/7 National&nbsp;
Helpline at 1-800-662-HELP. That's 1-800-662-4357.

